save('P0342','fringe-clt-uniform-error-threshold','Quantitative sufficient condition for transferring a leaf-sum CLT',
'''Let X_n and Y_n be integrable sums of n leaf scores on one coupled probability space. Suppose every corresponding score differs in absolute value by at most epsilon_n, and sqrt(n)*epsilon_n tends to zero. If (Y_n-E Y_n)/sqrt(n) converges in distribution to N(0,sigma^2), then (X_n-E X_n)/sqrt(n) has the same limit. In particular, a sufficient quantitative locality condition is a coupling of finite-tree scores to the stationary scores with uniform score error o(n^(-1/2)).''',
'''The pathwise sum difference is bounded by n*epsilon_n and the difference of the expectations by the same quantity. Hence the absolute difference of the centered normalized sums is at most 2*sqrt(n)*epsilon_n, which tends to zero. For any real x and eta>0, their distribution functions are squeezed between the distribution function of the Y sum at x-eta and x+eta for all sufficiently large n. Continuity of the Gaussian distribution function proves the assertion, including a constant limit by the usual convergence-in-probability version. This is an explicit transfer criterion, not a proof that the beta-splitting model supplies the coupling.''',
['Integrable scores and a common coupling','A deterministic uniform per-score error bound','sqrt(n)*epsilon_n -> 0'],
'No such coupling or error bound has been proved here for DTCS(n). A stationary CLT and qualitative local convergence alone do not provide it.')

save('P0342','retained-root-fringe-perturbation-threshold','Three fluctuation regimes for the earlier retained-root perturbation',
'''Assume finite binary trees retain an identifiable original degree-two root after leaf rerooting, and their ordered root split law is q_n(i)=n/(2 H_(n-1) i(n-i)), 1<=i<n. Let B_n indicate min(i,n-i)<=floor(sqrt(n)), let p_n=E B_n, and give every leaf the same score f_a=n^(-a)(B_n-p_n), with score zero on infinite trees and a>0. The finite centered score sum divided by sqrt(n) is n^(1/2-a)(B_n-p_n). It is not tight for 0<a<1/2, converges to a two-point law at a=1/2, and tends uniformly to zero for a>1/2. Every infinite-tree score is zero.''',
'''For n>=9 put k=floor(sqrt(n))<n/2. Since q_n(i)=(1/i+1/(n-i))/(2 H_(n-1)), symmetry gives p_n=(H_k+H_(n-1)-H_(n-k-1))/H_(n-1). The first numerator term is (1/2) log n+O(1), and the other difference is at most k/(n-k)=O(n^(-1/2)); H_(n-1)=log n+O(1). Thus p_n ->1/2. Both possible values of B_n-p_n have absolute value approaching 1/2. Multiplication by n^(1/2-a) gives the three stated regimes. The scores have expectation zero exactly. On bounded-degree trees with the retained-root topology, every finite tree is isolated locally, while convergence to an infinite tree forces the leaf count to diverge. Therefore |f_a|<=n^(-a) proves continuity at infinite trees. This generalizes the earlier a=1/4 obstruction; it does not verify the retained-root assumption in the original problem's precise topology.''',
['The original degree-two root is retained and identifiable','The displayed critical root-split law','The local topology and score domain retain that root','a>0'],
'This is a conditional topology-specific obstruction with a degenerate infinite CLT. It is not a checked solution of Open Problem 12. A concrete nondegenerate beta-splitting counterexample remains open in this packet.')

save('C0057','survival-compensator-exhaustion-criterion','Discovery from a divergent conditional survival compensator',
'''Let T>=1 be an integer-valued stopping time for a filtration (F_n), possibly infinite, and set p_n=E[1_{T=n+1}|F_n] and A_m=sum_(n<m) p_n. Then P(T=infinity and A_infinity=infinity)=0. For countably many targets this implies almost sure discovery of all targets if each target's compensator diverges on its never-discovered event outside a null set. Divergence is a sufficient condition, not a necessary one.''',
'''Because p_n=0 after T, Z_m=1_{T>m} exp(A_m) is a nonnegative supermartingale with Z_0=1: its conditional next expectation is Z_m*(1-p_m)*exp(p_m)<=Z_m. For K>0 stop at sigma_K=inf{m:A_m>=K}. Bounded optional stopping at sigma_K wedge r gives P(sigma_K<=r,T>sigma_K)<=exp(-K). Let r increase, and then K increase, to obtain the result. The countable-target conclusion follows by a countable union of null sets. If deterministic a_n in [0,1] satisfy p_n>=a_n on {T>n}, the direct recursion P(T>n+1)<= (1-a_n)P(T>n) gives the product tail bound and hence P(T>m)<=exp(-sum_(n<m)a_n). For a_n=c/(n+1), 0<c<=1, this is at most (m+1)^(-c). Necessity fails when T=1 deterministically, since A_infinity=1 although discovery is certain.''',
['T is a stopping time relative to the actual exploration history','p_n are actual target-specific conditional probabilities','The countable application verifies divergence separately on each survival event'],
'For filled-in peeling, the lower bound by a perimeter-only q(S_n) and the divergence for all critical weight sequences are still required. The earlier unreviewed equivalence is not imported as a theorem.')

save('P0024','annular-skoro-distance-expansion','Exact finite-time expansion in the annular Skorokhod problem',
'''For the smooth bounded connected annulus D={x in R^2:1<|x|<3}, normal reflection driven by w(t)=(-t,0) can increase the distance of two synchronously driven paths. Start X(0)=(1,0) and Y(0)=(cos(theta),sin(theta)), 0<theta<pi/2. Until t<log(cot(theta/2)), X(t)=(1,0) and Y(t)=(cos(phi(t)),sin(phi(t))) with phi(t)=2 arctan(exp(t)tan(theta/2)); the squared distance has derivative 2 sin(phi(t))^2>0.''',
'''On the inner circle the inward normal of the annulus is the outward radial vector. For X, take reflection regulator L_X(t)=t, so dX=(-1,0)dt+(1,0)dL_X=0. For Y, choose dL_Y=cos(phi)dt, which is nonnegative while phi<pi/2. The Skorokhod equation then gives Y'=(-1,0)+cos(phi)(cos(phi),sin(phi))=(-sin(phi)^2,cos(phi)sin(phi)). This equals (-sin(phi),cos(phi))*phi' when phi'=sin(phi), and the displayed solution solves that ODE. Both regulators increase only on the inner boundary, both paths stay in the closed annulus, and neither reaches the outer boundary. Their squared distance is 2(1-cos(phi)), with derivative 2 sin(phi)^2. This is a complete deterministic calculation.''',
['Deterministic continuous finite-variation driving path','Normal Skorokhod reflection on the closed annulus','Boundary starting points and 0<theta<pi/2','0<=t<log(cot(theta/2))'],
'Finite-time deterministic expansion does not establish positive long-time limsup for reflected Brownian motion. A Brownian support argument and recurrence of expansion episodes would be separate steps; neither is claimed here.')

save('P0218','critical-immigration-logarithmic-lower-bound','Logarithmic city-count lower bound under the earlier founding-rate estimate',
'''Let (M_t) be an adapted nondecreasing integer process, t>=1, with M_1>=1, whose increments are zero or one. Suppose P(M_(t+1)=M_t+1|F_t)>=1/[2(1+K*t*M_t^d)] for constants K>0,d>0. Then constants kappa,c>0 exist such that, for all large integer t, P(M_t<floor(kappa*(log t)^(1/(d+1))))<=exp(-c*(log t)^(1/(d+1))). Moreover almost surely liminf M_t/(log t)^(1/(d+1))>0. Under the earlier city-model founding estimate at alpha=1 and beta>2, d=beta/2-1, giving exponent 2/beta.''',
'''Set m=floor(kappa*(log t)^(1/(d+1)))>=1. Before M reaches m, the founding chance at step s is at least a_s=1/[2(1+K*s*m^d)]>=1/[2(1+K)*s*m^d] for s>=1. Using independent uniforms to realize the adapted Bernoulli increments, compare with independent Bernoulli(a_s) variables until the threshold; after the threshold extend the comparison success variables arbitrarily. If M_t<m then the threshold was never reached, so the sum B of comparison successes from s=1 to t-1 is smaller than m. Its mean mu is at least H_(t-1)/[2(1+K)*m^d]. Choose kappa so kappa^(d+1)<=1/[16(1+K)]. For large t the harmonic sum is at least (log t)/2, implying mu>=4m and mu is bounded below by a positive multiple of (log t)^(1/(d+1)). The Chernoff lower-tail bound P(B<=mu/2)<=exp(-mu/8) proves the probability assertion. For the almost sure statement use t_j=ceil(exp(j^(d+1))) and sum the bounds exp(-c*j). Borel-Cantelli gives the lower bound eventually on these times. Monotonicity of M_t and log(t_(j+1))/log(t_j)->1 interpolate it to all large t.''',
['Adapted city-count process with increments in {0,1}','The displayed conditional founding-rate bound at every step','K>0 and d>0'],
'The process lemma is proved. Its application to the spatial immigration model is conditional on checking the earlier disk-area bound against the primary model formula. This does not give the matching upper bound or comparable city populations.')
